export const meta = {
  name: 'expense-guard-audit',
  description: 'Read-only 4-axis audit of the Expense Guard agent, each finding adversarially refuted',
  phases: [
    { title: 'Find', detail: 'one finder per axis: correctness, tenant-security, cost, maintainability' },
    { title: 'Refute', detail: 'one skeptic per finding — real bug or framework idiom?' },
  ],
}

const REPO = '/Users/axl-devhub/repos/expense-guard-challenge'

const GROUND_RULES = `
You are auditing the Expense Guard codebase at ${REPO}.

STRICTLY READ-ONLY. You must NOT:
  - edit, create, or delete any file (no Edit/Write/NotebookEdit)
  - start, stop, or restart any server
  - POST to the running dev server on 127.0.0.1:2000

That last rule matters: a dev server IS currently running and its in-memory policy
cache is already in a poisoned state. Sending requests would both cost gateway
credits and give you misleading results. Reason statically instead.

You MAY: read files, grep, run \`bunx tsc --noEmit\`, read node_modules/eve to check
framework contracts, and read .eve/compile/compiled-agent-manifest.json.

CONTEXT — what this is:
An Eve (Vercel agent framework, v0.11.7) agent that reviews expense submissions for a
multi-tenant platform. POST /eve/v1/review -> agent/channels/eve.ts parses the body into
a request view (agent/lib/request-context.ts) and seeds it as channel state -> the
instructions resolver (agent/instructions/system.ts) resolves the submission from channel
metadata, seeds submissionState, renders the system prompt (agent/lib/build-instructions.ts)
-> the model calls tools (agent/tools/search_policy.ts, agent/tools/validate_expense.ts;
every other file in agent/tools/ is disableTool(), which is a CORRECT pattern, not a bug)
-> output validated against ExpenseDecisionSchema (agent/lib/expense.schema.ts).
Policies live in agent/lib/policies.ts behind agent/lib/policy-store.ts.
The repo deliberately contains planted bugs. The brief's ground rule: multi-tenant
isolation is sacred, and cost is a design constraint.

ALREADY CONFIRMED — do NOT spend findings on these three, they are proven and being fixed:
  1. agent/lib/policy-store.ts getCompanyPolicy() memoizes into a module-level unkeyed
     \`activePolicy\`; first company looked up in the process wins forever. Proven live,
     leaks in both directions depending on request order.
  2. agent/lib/build-instructions.ts puts the volatile submission block BEFORE the static
     rubric, so there is no cacheable shared prefix. Measured: cacheReadTokens 0 on all
     10 observed steps.
  3. agent/channels/eve.ts is named "eve", shadowing Eve's built-in channel of the same
     name, so /eve/v1/session never registers and \`bunx eve eval\` 404s before any
     assertion runs.

Find what ELSE is wrong. Novel findings only. Depth over breadth.
Report at most 3 findings, ranked most severe first. Fewer is fine — do not pad.
If you can only find one real thing, report one.
`

